# Explore Strange Loops

A strange loop appears when a proposition says exactly that it is not certified: $g\leftrightarrow
eg P(g)$. Begin with the complete finite census, where every possible unary Boolean operator can be inspected.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## The two indispensable ingredients

The incompleteness conclusion needs both a diagonal fixed point and reflection, the principle $P(a)\Rightarrow a$. The dependency map makes their roles visible.

{{visualization:2}}

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the complete reflected fixed-point argument</summary>

Assume $P(g)$. Reflection gives $g$. The equation $g\leftrightarrow
eg P(g)$ then gives $
eg P(g)$, contradiction. Therefore $
eg P(g)$; applying the reverse implication gives $g$. Thus $g$ is true and uncertified. If all truths were certified, $P(g)$ would follow, so semantic completeness fails.

</details>

## Why neither hypothesis is automatic

Use the stress test to remove one assumption at a time. Identity shows that monotonicity does not create a diagonal sentence. Constant truth shows that a loop can coexist with syntactic completeness when reflection and consistency are absent.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:1}}

For background, see the [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on Gödel's incompleteness theorems](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goedel-incompleteness/) and the [Encyclopedia of Mathematics entry on fixed points](https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Fixed_point).

## The order-theoretic picture

Propositions are ordered by implication. If $P$ is monotone, $a\mapsto
eg P(a)$ reverses that order. Its fixed points form an antichain modulo logical equivalence.

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the classical antichain proof</summary>

Let $g$ and $h$ be fixed points and suppose $g\Rightarrow h$. If $h$ holds, then $
eg P(h)$. If $g$ failed, classical double-negation elimination and $g\leftrightarrow
eg P(g)$ would give $P(g)$. Monotonicity would then give $P(h)$, contradiction. Hence $h\Rightarrow g$, so $g\leftrightarrow h$.

</details>

## Philosophical boundary

Self-reference is relevant to self-modeling, feedback, and metacognition, but no result here defines or explains consciousness. A scientific bridge would need an explicit agent model and an operationally testable predicate. The mathematics establishes a boundary between truth and certification—not a theory of subjective experience.

